!! Real-world example: SaaS product technology strategy

This example maps the key components of a SaaS product, showing which to build, buy, or outsource based on their position on the evolution axis. Components close to `Genesis` (left) are novel and warrant investment; components near `Commodity` (right) are best purchased or outsourced.

```
wardley-beta
    %% ── Canvas and accessibility ─────────────────
    title SaaS Product Technology Strategy
    accTitle: SaaS product Wardley Map
    accDescr: Wardley Map showing technology strategy for a SaaS product

    %% ── Customer anchor ──────────────────────────
    anchor Customer [0.95, 0.95]

    %% ── User-visible components ──────────────────
    component Dashboard [0.85, 0.55] (buy)
    component Authentication [0.75, 0.85] (outsource)
    component Notifications [0.65, 0.70] (buy)

    %% ── Core platform components ─────────────────
    component Business Logic [0.55, 0.30] (build)
    component Data Pipeline [0.40, 0.35] (build)
    component API Gateway [0.50, 0.60] (buy)

    %% ── Infrastructure components ────────────────
    component PostgreSQL [0.30, 0.80] (outsource)
    component Object Storage [0.25, 0.90] (outsource)
    component Compute [0.20, 0.85] (outsource)

    %% ── Dependency links ─────────────────────────
    Customer -> Dashboard
    Dashboard -> Business Logic
    Customer -> Authentication
    Customer -> Notifications
    Notifications -> Business Logic
    Business Logic -> API Gateway
    API Gateway -> Data Pipeline
    Data Pipeline -> PostgreSQL
    Data Pipeline -> Object Storage
    Business Logic -> Compute

    %% ── Planned evolution ────────────────────────
    evolve Business Logic 0.55
```

$$$text/vnd.tiddlywiki.mermaid
wardley-beta
